{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at 59f8a221538537cfef54caf5dd158bdcd9206e8e on request/commitment-honesty.\n\nA first artifact at this path. Nothing live covered internal/statusview before, which is itself worth noting: the package owns the lane classification and the bounded views, and no documentation could flare when they moved because there was nothing at this string to supersede.\n\nWhat this head puts here. The terminal lane holds satisfied and withdrawn and deliberately excludes both third-party retirements. Terminal rows are dropped from the bounded lists entirely, so filing an authority's removal of someone else's request or promise as terminal would make it the quietest event on the board. released is additionally not actionable: the promise it names is gone, so treating its row as active would double-count once a replacement promise exists, and it is the live request that wants one — from the actor it is already addressed to, not from a new performer.\n\nThe lane-table test carries a note about its own limits. It mirrors the fold's vocabulary by hand, so it catches a lane naming a status the fold cannot emit and cannot catch the fold emitting a status no lane names. Request 1f859eff asks for one owner of this vocabulary, which would remove the need for the mirror.\n\nPublished so the documentation that describes these lanes has something at this exact path to rest on. docs/reference/gs/status.md and the MCP status pages describe behaviour that lives here and have been resting on artifacts for other packages.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc, the browser tests and ui-check all pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"branch":"request/commitment-honesty","commit":"59f8a221538537cfef54caf5dd158bdcd9206e8e","head":"59f8a221538537cfef54caf5dd158bdcd9206e8e","path":"internal/statusview"}}